        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Work dry first: scrape and sweep on a rain-free day, then kill the remainder with a single product used by its label, and lift the dead growth with a broom or a light, wide-tip pressure washer pass. Finish by filling cracks and resealing worn pavement so rain runs off rather than soaking in.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            What keeps moss away is a change of conditions, not a stronger spray. Give the driveway more sun, move the water off it and keep it clean and sealed, and a moss-prone drive becomes one that needs only a quick sweep each season.
          </p>
